The main difference between B-trees and B+ trees lies in their internal organization and how they handle data.

1. **B-Trees (Variable Splitting)****
   B-trees are designed to maintain a balance between keys and leaf nodes. When a key exceeds the capacity of a node, the tree splits the node into two or more child nodes.

   This process can lead to variable branching in the tree, where some branches have only one child while others have multiple children.

2. **B+ Trees (Static Splitting)****
   B+ trees are a variation of B-trees that strive for better cache performance by ensuring all leaf nodes are stored on a single disk page.

   In this case, when a key exceeds the capacity of a node, the tree statically splits the node into two or more child nodes. This ensures that all leaf nodes are consecutive in memory, which is beneficial for caching and random access.

In summary, B-trees use variable splitting to maintain balance, while B+ trees employ static splitting to optimize cache performance.
